# Executive Summary
**Thesis:** Our legacy SOAP APIs are creating a bottleneck for real-time data integration. Modernizing to REST with event streaming will enable faster feature delivery and reduce operational burden.

**Expected Business Impact:**
- Reduce API integration time from 4 weeks to 1 week per new consumer
- Enable real-time data flows (currently batch-only, 24-hour latency)
- Reduce API-related production incidents by 40%
- Estimated value: $2M annually in engineering efficiency + $500K in operational costs saved

**Timeline:** 6 months from approval to full migration

**Investment Required:** $800K (team), $150K (tools/infrastructure), $100K contingency

---

# Business Context

## Problem Statement
- **What:** Our 15-year-old SOAP API stack handles 80% of our integrations but is brittle, hard to extend, and requires 2-4 weeks per new consumer onboarding
- **Why it matters:** We're losing enterprise deals because integration takes too long. Competitors can onboard them in days.
- **Scope:** SOAP APIs only (not internal APIs, not mobile); 12 key consumers; batch and real-time data flows
- **Current state:**
  - 45 active SOAP endpoints
  - 8-10 new consumer requests per quarter (70% take >3 weeks to integrate)
  - 2-3 production incidents per month related to SOAP/XML parsing
  - 5 FTE maintaining infrastructure

## Stakeholders & Influencers
- **Primary decision-maker:** VP Engineering (cares about delivery velocity, wants to reduce toil)
- **Key stakeholders:** Enterprise Sales (wants faster onboarding), Product (wants real-time data), Operations (maintenance burden)
- **Allies:** Principal Engineer (has been vocal about modernization), Platform team (wants to retire old stack)
- **Resistors:** Legacy Systems Owner (concerned about stability), Finance (wants to see clear ROI before approving)
- **Success criteria owner:** VP Engineering (tracks integration time, incident metrics)

---

# Proposed Solution

## Architecture Overview
Replace SOAP with REST + Event Streaming architecture. New integrations use REST APIs for synchronous calls and Kafka for real-time data. Legacy SOAP consumers get a bridge layer for 12 months of coexistence.

## How It Works
1. **Phase 1:** Deploy REST API gateway and event streaming infrastructure (Kafka)
2. **Phase 2:** Build REST versions of top 5 SOAP endpoints; migrate 3 key consumers
3. **Phase 3:** Migrate remaining 9 consumers from SOAP to REST
4. **Phase 4:** Decommission SOAP stack; support bridge layer only
5. **Phase 5:** Retire bridge layer (12 months after Phase 1)

## Integration Points
- Connects to 12 external systems (each needs migration plan)
- Internal data warehouse depends on current batch flows (must maintain data SLAs)
- Security team needs to validate REST auth model (OAuth 2.0)

## Strategic Alignment
- Aligns with "Modernize Legacy Systems" 2026 strategic priority
- Enables "Real-Time Analytics" product roadmap for Q4 2026
- Reduces tech debt and operational risk

---

# Trade-off Analysis

| Aspect | REST + Kafka (Proposed) | Keep SOAP + Improve | Lift-and-Shift to gRPC |
|--------|------------------------|-------------------|------------------------|
| **Cost** | $1.05M | $200K | $1.2M |
| **Timeline** | 6 months | 2 months (limited benefit) | 5 months |
| **Consumer adoption** | Fast (REST is standard) | Medium (SOAP still friction) | Slow (gRPC not common) |
| **Real-time capability** | Yes (Kafka) | No | No |
| **Skills available** | Abundant (REST/Kafka common) | Declining (SOAP expertise aging) | Medium (gRPC emerging) |
| **Why chosen** | Fastest path to business value; unblocks real-time product roadmap; lower risk (mainstream tech) | Doesn't solve real-time need; wastes money on dead tech | Higher risk; skills gap; doesn't align with industry direction |

---

# Risk & Mitigation

| Risk | Likelihood | Impact | Mitigation | Owner | Target Date |
|------|-----------|--------|-----------|-------|------------|
| Consumer resistance to change | Medium | High | Early engagement with consumer teams; REST is industry standard so lower resistance expected | VP Engineering | Week 2 |
| Data consistency during migration | Medium | High | Dual-write strategy for 6-month transition period; validation testing for each consumer | Platform Lead | Week 4 |
| Kafka operational burden | Low | Medium | Hire Kafka specialist; adopt managed Kafka service (reduces ops burden) | Ops Lead | Week 3 |
| Security team delays auth review | Low | High | Engage security in Week 1; use industry-standard OAuth 2.0 (not novel) | Security Lead | Week 1 |
| Consumer discovery of new API | Medium | Medium | Publish API catalog; hold webinars; provide migration guide and support | Dev Advocate | Week 6 |

---

# Implementation Plan

## Phase 1: Planning & Infrastructure (Weeks 1-8)
- **Deliverables:** REST API design spec, Kafka infrastructure, OAuth 2.0 implementation, migration playbook for each consumer
- **Dependencies:** Security team approval, infrastructure team capacity
- **Owners:** Platform Lead (overall), Architect (design), Ops (infrastructure)
- **Success criteria:** Design approved by security and key stakeholders; infrastructure running in staging

## Phase 2: Build & Test REST APIs (Weeks 9-16)
- **Deliverables:** REST versions of 5 top endpoints, test coverage, documentation
- **Dependencies:** Phase 1 complete; consumer teams available for testing
- **Owners:** 2 Backend engineers, QA
- **Success criteria:** 3 pilot consumers successfully testing; REST API meets performance targets (p95 <100ms)

## Phase 3: Migrate Pilot Consumers (Weeks 17-24)
- **Deliverables:** 3 consumers migrated to REST; bridge layer operational
- **Dependencies:** Phase 2 complete; pilot consumers committed to migration
- **Owners:** Integration team, Platform team
- **Success criteria:** All 3 pilots in production; zero data loss; integration time reduced to 1-2 weeks per consumer

## Phase 4: Full Rollout (Weeks 25-28)
- **Deliverables:** Remaining 9 consumers migrated; SOAP stack decommissioned
- **Dependencies:** Phase 3 successful; all consumers trained
- **Owners:** Platform team, Operations
- **Success criteria:** 12/12 consumers migrated; SOAP stack removed from production

---

# Resource Requirements

## Team Composition
- **Architecture Lead:** 50% time (design, decisions, stakeholder alignment)
- **Backend Engineers:** 2 FTE (implementation, testing)
- **Platform/Infra:** 1 FTE (Kafka setup, bridge layer, ops)
- **QA:** 0.5 FTE (test strategy, validation)
- **Product/Advocacy:** 0.5 FTE (consumer communication, migration support)

## Budget Breakdown
- **Personnel:** $800K (6 months @ blended rate for team)
- **Kafka infrastructure:** $80K (managed service, compute, storage)
- **Tools/licenses:** $40K (API management platform, monitoring)
- **Training/consulting:** $30K (external Kafka expert, OAuth specialist)
- **Contingency (10%):** $95K
- **Total:** $1.045M

---

# Success Metrics & Measurement

## Primary Success Measures
1. **Consumer Integration Time**
   - Target: 1 week per new consumer (from 4 weeks)
   - Baseline: 27 days average
   - Measurement: Track from request to go-live, monthly
   - Owner: VP Engineering
   - Success threshold: 80% of new consumers integrated in ≤7 days by month 6

2. **Real-Time Data Capability**
   - Target: Enable sub-5-minute data latency
   - Baseline: 24-hour batch latency
   - Measurement: Data freshness in warehouse
   - Owner: Analytics team
   - Success threshold: Product can launch real-time dashboard by Q4 2026

3. **Production Incidents**
   - Target: ≤0.5 incidents per month (from 2-3 per month)
   - Baseline: 2.5 incidents/month
   - Measurement: Incident tracking system
   - Owner: Operations
   - Success threshold: Month 6-12 post-launch: <0.5 incidents/month

4. **Consumer Adoption**
   - Target: 100% of active consumers migrated
   - Baseline: 0%
   - Measurement: Consumer count on REST vs. SOAP
   - Owner: Product team
   - Success threshold: 12/12 consumers on REST by end of Phase 4

---

# Communication & Stakeholder Management

## Approval Gates
1. **Gate 1 (Week 1):** Executive sponsor approves investment and timeline
2. **Gate 2 (Week 8):** Architecture and design approved by tech steering committee
3. **Gate 3 (Week 16):** Pilot results accepted; greenlight for full rollout
4. **Gate 4 (Week 28):** SOAP stack decommissioned; close-out

## Communication Plan
- **Week 0:** Executive stakeholder alignment (VP Eng, VP Sales, Finance)
- **Week 1:** Announce to all consumer teams + Q&A session
- **Week 8:** Share design and migration timeline
- **Week 16:** Celebrate pilot wins; share learnings
- **Week 28:** Retirement party for SOAP stack (yes, really — this builds goodwill)

